      <text><![CDATA[<p>Let \(f(x)={@func@}\)</p>
<br>\(f ^{ \prime} (x)={@f_x@} \implies f^{ \prime} (0)={@f_x_0@}\)
<br>\(f^{ \prime \prime }(x)={@f1_x@} \implies f ^{ \prime \prime } (0)={@f1_x_0@}\)
<br>\(f^{ \prime \prime \prime } (x)={@f2_x@} \implies f ^{ \prime \prime \prime } (0)={@f2_x_0@}\)
<br>\(f^{ \prime \prime \prime \prime }(x)={@f3_x@} \implies f ^{ \prime \prime \prime \prime  } (0)={@f3_x_0@}\)
<br><b>Step</b> \(2:\) Use the Maclaurin series (special case of the Taylor series) to determine \({@func@}\) up to order \(4\),
<p>\({@func@}={@ta@}\)</p>]]></text>
